{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AzoGR6LkShcDkKtJcGhZrw","type":"tool_result","content":"     */\n    refreshAssets(): Promise<void>;\n    /**\n     * Establishes (omit `limit`) or removes (`limit: '0'`) a trustline for an asset.\n     *\n     * The app config decides who pays, server-side - the SDK never pre-decides.\n     * Sponsorship is **on by default** (the app covers the 0.5 XLM reserve + fee\n     * when eligible); pass `skipSponsorship` to force the user's own wallet to pay,\n     * mirroring the opt-out on the payment / swap / contract fee-bump surfaces. The\n     * route is by wallet type, and each server endpoint sponsors-or-self-pays:\n     *  - **Embedded** (internal wallet, no adapter) -> one call to\n     *    `/wallet/assets/trustline`: the server signs the way the wallet's signer\n     *    allows. A custodial wallet is sponsored or self-paid and submitted there,\n     *    returning the refreshed asset list; a co-signed one answers with the\n     *    request its device approves, settled like any other co-signed send.\n     *  - **External/adapter** -> `/wallet/assets/trustline/build` returns a\n     *    `changeTrust` XDR (sponsor-signed when covered, or a plain self-pay one\n     *    otherwise); the user's own wallet adds the trustor signature and submits.\n     *  - **`skipSponsorship`** -> a plain self-pay `change_trust` via {@link runTx},\n     *    bypassing the sponsoring endpoints for both wallet types.\n     *\n     * Does not refresh on its own - callers should `refreshAssets()` afterwards.\n     */\n    setTrustline(asset: {\n        code: string;\n        issuer: string;\n    }, opts?: {\n        limit?: string;\n        /**\n         * Force self-pay even when the app would sponsor the trustline - the\n         * opt-out that mirrors `skipSponsorship` on the payment / swap / contract\n         * fee-bump surfaces.\n         */\n        skipSponsorship?: boolean;\n    }): Promise<TrustlineOutcome>;\n    /** A refused trustline call, with the API's code so a UI can say what to do about it. */\n    private _trustlineError;\n    /**\n     * Create this wallet's account on the Stellar network when it doesn't exist\n     * yet. For EXTERNAL wallets (Freighter / client-side Privy) whose key the\n     * platform doesn't hold: the server builds a sponsored `createAccount` (the new\n     * account starts at \"0\" balance; the app's sponsor wallet pays the base reserve\n     * and fee) and signs only the sponsor. This client adds the new-account\n     * signature with the user's own wallet and broadcasts it via the submit path.\n     *\n     * Not applicable to embedded (internal) wallets - those are created on the\n     * server at login - nor to smart (C-address) wallets, which don't use classic\n     * accounts. Trustlines are a separate step: see {@link setTrustline}.\n     */\n    createAccount(): Promise<SubmitOutcome>;\n    /**\n     * Builds an unsigned XDR. Drives `_setTransactionState` for modal-style UIs\n     * AND returns a {@link BuildOutcome} so headless callers can `await` and\n     * inspect the result without subscribing to state changes.\n     */\n    buildTx(operation: TxBuildBody['operation'], params: TxBuildBody['params'], options?: TxBuildBody['options']): Promise<BuildOutcome>;\n----\n            address: string;\n        } | null;\n    };\n}\ninterface PollarClientConfig {\n    stellarNetwork?: StellarNetwork;\n    baseUrl?: string;\n    apiKey: string;\n    /**\n     * Pluggable storage. Defaults to `defaultStorage()` on web (localStorage\n     * with memory fallback). On RN you must inject one of the adapters from\n     * `@pollar/core/adapters/expo` or `@pollar/core/adapters/react-native-keychain`.\n     */\n    storage?: Storage;\n    /**\n     * Max time (ms) a single SDK HTTP attempt waits before aborting and rejecting\n     * with a {@link PollarNetworkError} (`code: 'SDK_NETWORK_TIMEOUT'`).\n     *\n     * `fetch` has no timeout of its own, so without this a transient connection\n     * stall (e.g. a dropped TCP SYN on a flaky mobile network at cold start) hangs\n     * the request forever - it neither resolves nor rejects, trapping any caller\n     * that `await`s it (a returning user stuck on the splash screen). Bounding it\n     * lets the request fail fast so the caller can recover (retry, or fall back to\n     * a cached token).\n     *\n     * Defaults to `10000` (10s). Set `0` to disable (NOT recommended - restores\n     * the unbounded-hang behavior).\n     */\n    requestTimeoutMs?: number;\n    /**\n     * Per-request timeout (ms) applied to the submit-family tx calls\n----\n * describe trustlines and stay Stellar-only; `decimals` is carried by Polygon and\n * Solana tokens, which need it to format raw amounts.\n *\n * `balance` is null when the chain could not be read - an unreachable RPC, not an\n * empty wallet. Render it as unavailable rather than as zero.\n */\ninterface WalletBalanceRecord {\n    chain?: WalletChain;\n    type?: 'native' | 'token' | 'credit_alphanum4' | 'credit_alphanum12';\n    code: string;\n    issuer?: string;\n    decimals?: number;\n    balance: string | null;\n    available: string | null;\n    limit?: string;\n    enabledInApp?: boolean;\n    trustlineRemoved?: boolean;\n    /** Solana only: the app fronts this token's transfer fee (`sponsor_transfer`). */\n    sponsored?: boolean;\n}\n/**\n * The authenticated user's balances. v2 is multichain: `balances` is the flat\n * list of assets across every chain the app is provisioned on, each tagged with\n * its `chain`. `multichain` is true when more than one chain is present, which is\n * the signal the UI uses to show a per-asset network tag. `exists` is true when\n * any chain holds an account/balance; `network` / `publicKey` reflect the Stellar\n * wallet (the primary chain).\n */\ninterface WalletBalanceContent {\n    publicKey: string;\n    network: string;\n    exists: boolean;\n    multichain?: boolean;\n    balances: WalletBalanceRecord[];\n}\n/** `GET /wallet/balance` as v2 answers it: one entry per chain, or an `error` for a chain it could not read. */\n----\n    step: 'error';\n    params: TxHistoryParams;\n    message: string;\n};\ntype KycLevel = 'basic' | 'intermediate' | 'enhanced';\ntype KycStatus = 'none' | 'pending' | 'approved' | 'rejected' | 'expired';\ntype KycDecisionStatus = 'pending' | 'manual_review' | 'approved' | 'rejected' | 'expired';\ntype KycFlow = 'iframe' | 'form' | 'redirect';\n/** One read of GET /kyc/status. `status` is what gates the user; `decisionStatus`\n *  and `reviewReason` say why a `pending` is pending (e.g. held for manual review). */\ntype KycStatusContent = paths['/kyc/status']['get']['responses'][200]['content']['application/json']['content'];\ntype KycProvider = paths['/kyc/providers']['get']['responses'][200]['content']['application/json']['content']['providers'][number];\ntype KycStartBody = NonNullable<paths['/kyc/start']['post']['requestBody']>['content']['application/json'];\ntype KycStartResponse = paths['/kyc/start']['post']['responses'][200]['content']['application/json']['content'];\n/** Closed ramp chain catalog, independent of wallet networks. */\ndeclare const RampChain: Readonly<{\n---\n    submitRequirementForm(formId: string, answers: RequirementFormAnswers): Promise<RequirementFormSubmitted>;\n    getRegistryCheck(optionId: string): Promise<RegistryCheck>;\n    submitRegistryCheck(optionId: string, edit: RegistryCheckEdit): Promise<RegistryCheckSubmitted>;\n    getProviderRegistration(corridorId: string): Promise<ProviderRegistration>;\n    submitProviderRegistration(corridorId: string): Promise<ProviderRegistrationSubmitted>;\n    /** With `corridorId` or `cardProviderId` the read is scoped to that route's or provider's steps. */\n    getKycStatus(providerId?: string, corridorId?: string, cardProviderId?: string): Promise<{\n        status: \"none\" | \"pending\" | \"approved\" | \"rejected\" | \"expired\";\n        level?: \"basic\" | \"intermediate\" | \"enhanced\";\n        providerId?: string;\n        decisionStatus?: \"pending\" | \"manual_review\" | \"approved\" | \"rejected\" | \"expired\";\n        expiresAt?: string;\n        reviewReason?: string;\n        pendingStep?: {\n            position: number;\n            type: \"KYC\" | \"FORM\" | \"REGISTRY_CHECK\" | \"PROVIDER_REGISTRATION\";\n            optionId: string;\n        };\n    }>;\n    getKycProviders(country: string, corridorId?: string, cardProviderId?: string): Promise<{\n        providers: KycProvider[];\n    }>;\n    startKyc(body: KycStartBody & {\n        cardProviderId?: string;\n    }): Promise<KycStartResponse>;\n    resolveKyc(providerId: string, level?: KycLevel, country?: string, corridorId?: string, idempotencyKey?: string, cardProviderId?: string): Promise<{\n        alreadyApproved: boolean;\n    } & Partial<{\n        sessionId: string;\n        kycUrl?: string;\n        fields?: {\n            name: string;\n            type: string;\n            required: boolean;\n        }[];\n    }>>;\n    pollKycDecision(providerId: string, opts?: {\n        intervalMs?: number;\n        timeoutMs?: number;\n        corridorId?: string;\n        cardProviderId?: string;\n    }): Promise<KycStatusContent>;\n    pollKycStatus(providerId: string, opts?: {\n        intervalMs?: number;\n        timeoutMs?: number;\n        corridorId?: string;\n        cardProviderId?: string;\n    }): Promise<KycStatus>;\n    /** The fiat and asset pairs the app can ramp between in a direction. Nothing is quoted. */\n    getRampRoutes(direction: RampDirection): Promise<RampRoutesResponse>;\n    /** Every route between the fiat and the asset, quoted leg by leg, best first. */","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4bce8851-e926-4120-9d22-c63185adba0d","uuid":"4b4208e7-709c-4113-af60-9d66a153b10c","timestamp":"2026-11-09T22:26:23.164Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"     */\n    refreshAssets(): Promise<void>;\n    /**\n     * Establishes (omit `limit`) or removes (`limit: '0'`) a trustline for an asset.\n     *\n     * The app config decides who pays, server-side - the SDK never pre-decides.\n     * Sponsorship is **on by default** (the app covers the 0.5 XLM reserve + fee\n     * when eligible); pass `skipSponsorship` to force the user's own wallet to pay,\n     * mirroring the opt-out on the payment / swap / contract fee-bump surfaces. The\n     * route is by wallet type, and each server endpoint sponsors-or-self-pays:\n     *  - **Embedded** (internal wallet, no adapter) -> one call to\n     *    `/wallet/assets/trustline`: the server signs the way the wallet's signer\n     *    allows. A custodial wallet is sponsored or self-paid and submitted there,\n     *    returning the refreshed asset list; a co-signed one answers with the\n     *    request its device approves, settled like any other co-signed send.\n     *  - **External/adapter** -> `/wallet/assets/trustline/build` returns a\n     *    `changeTrust` XDR (sponsor-signed when covered, or a plain self-pay one\n     *    otherwise); the user's own wallet adds the trustor signature and submits.\n     *  - **`skipSponsorship`** -> a plain self-pay `change_trust` via {@link runTx},\n     *    bypassing the sponsoring endpoints for both wallet types.\n     *\n     * Does not refresh on its own - callers should `refreshAssets()` afterwards.\n     */\n    setTrustline(asset: {\n        code: string;\n        issuer: string;\n    }, opts?: {\n        limit?: string;\n        /**\n         * Force self-pay even when the app would sponsor the trustline - the\n         * opt-out that mirrors `skipSponsorship` on the payment / swap / contract\n         * fee-bump surfaces.\n         */\n        skipSponsorship?: boolean;\n    }): Promise<TrustlineOutcome>;\n    /** A refused trustline call, with the API's code so a UI can say what to do about it. */\n    private _trustlineError;\n    /**\n     * Create this wallet's account on the Stellar network when it doesn't exist\n     * yet. For EXTERNAL wallets (Freighter / client-side Privy) whose key the\n     * platform doesn't hold: the server builds a sponsored `createAccount` (the new\n     * account starts at \"0\" balance; the app's sponsor wallet pays the base reserve\n     * and fee) and signs only the sponsor. This client adds the new-account\n     * signature with the user's own wallet and broadcasts it via the submit path.\n     *\n     * Not applicable to embedded (internal) wallets - those are created on the\n     * server at login - nor to smart (C-address) wallets, which don't use classic\n     * accounts. Trustlines are a separate step: see {@link setTrustline}.\n     */\n    createAccount(): Promise<SubmitOutcome>;\n    /**\n     * Builds an unsigned XDR. Drives `_setTransactionState` for modal-style UIs\n     * AND returns a {@link BuildOutcome} so headless callers can `await` and\n     * inspect the result without subscribing to state changes.\n     */\n    buildTx(operation: TxBuildBody['operation'], params: TxBuildBody['params'], options?: TxBuildBody['options']): Promise<BuildOutcome>;\n----\n            address: string;\n        } | null;\n    };\n}\ninterface PollarClientConfig {\n    stellarNetwork?: StellarNetwork;\n    baseUrl?: string;\n    apiKey: string;\n    /**\n     * Pluggable storage. Defaults to `defaultStorage()` on web (localStorage\n     * with memory fallback). On RN you must inject one of the adapters from\n     * `@pollar/core/adapters/expo` or `@pollar/core/adapters/react-native-keychain`.\n     */\n    storage?: Storage;\n    /**\n     * Max time (ms) a single SDK HTTP attempt waits before aborting and rejecting\n     * with a {@link PollarNetworkError} (`code: 'SDK_NETWORK_TIMEOUT'`).\n     *\n     * `fetch` has no timeout of its own, so without this a transient connection\n     * stall (e.g. a dropped TCP SYN on a flaky mobile network at cold start) hangs\n     * the request